   130	repetitions = **24 runs per cell**, every episode with a per-step trace and a conditions
   131	header. Gemini's figures from the table above in parentheses.
   132	
   133	| T | ReAct | Memory | Stateful | SKILL.state |
   134	|---|---|---|---|---|
   135	| 50 | 0.978 ± 0.045 (0.936) | 0.809 ± 0.184 (0.953) | 0.991 ± 0.026 (0.996) | 0.999 ± 0.004 (1.000) |
   136	| 200 | 0.974 ± 0.070 (0.913) | 0.653 ± 0.157 (0.810) | **0.999 ± 0.002** (0.930) | **0.958 ± 0.058** (1.000) |
   137	
   138	Stateful cells are 15 runs (3 seeds × 5); the rest, 24. Haiku's Stateful needed a third
   139	parser version: the second still dropped the patches Haiku writes in Markdown
   140	(`**StateUpdate:**` followed by a code block) — 731 of 3,000 at T=200, in 7 of 15
   141	episodes — which an adversarial review found (§6, item 7). The cells above use the third,
   142	which applies 2,995 of 3,000 at T=200 and 747 of 750 at T=50; the remainder carry no
   143	JSON object or keys outside the schema.
   144	
   145	ReAct is flat on Haiku: −0.005 between T=50 and T=200, against −0.023 on Gemini and
   146	−0.140 in the paper over the same interval. The full-history arm the paper shows
   147	collapsing degrades on Haiku less than SKILL.state (−0.041); only Stateful degrades less.
   148	
   149	**SKILL.state fails on Haiku, and the adjudication says where.** We replay the
   150	environment with the actions the model executed and compare, step by step, the inventory
   151	the model believes (SKU, units and lot per shelf) against the real one
   152	(`experiments/adjudicar_estado.py`). A failure is attributed to the state only when the
   153	believed inventory prescribes a **different** action than the real one and the model
   154	executed exactly the action the believed inventory prescribes. Of the 201 failures at
   155	T=200:
   156	
   157	- **167 are caused by the state** in that sense; **17** occur while the belief differed
   158	  from reality but the discrepancy does not explain them (both inventories prescribe the
   159	  same action, or the model followed neither); **17** occur with a correct state.
   160	- **In all 17 episodes where the belief departed from reality, the origin is the same:
   161	  a `Move` executed correctly with a state patch written wrong.** `Move` is the only
   162	  transition that requires copying one shelf's contents into another key of the state,
   163	  and that copy is where it breaks — the model writes the contents of a different shelf,
   164	  the lot of the pallet it stored one step earlier, or the wrong number of units.
   165	- **Only in 7 of those 17 did the error reach a decision, all in seed 2.** In 6 of its
   166	  8 runs the corruption happens at the same step (132), after which the model ships from
   167	  a shelf that does not hold the SKU, cascades through ~25 state-caused failures and does
   168	  not correct its state even when the environment rejects the action. In seed 0 the
   169	  corrupted field is a lot number that no later decision reads: 8 corrupted runs, no
   170	  failure caused by it.
   171	
   172	What SKILL.state removes is the need to **reconstruct** the state from history. What it
   173	introduces is the need to **write** it correctly at every transition, and nothing in the
   174	runtime checks that the write matches what the action did. Gemini's belief never departed
   175	from reality in any of its 75 episodes; Haiku wrote at least one wrong patch in 17 of 24
   176	at T=200, and in 7 of them the error reached a decision. The paper's thesis survives in
   177	direction — SKILL.state is still the best arm on Gemini and within 0.02 of ReAct on
   178	Haiku — but "explicit state does not degrade" is a property of the model as much as of
   179	the runtime.
